Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Behaviours:
- Three levels decide whether a thread answers questions from linked
  machines; the nearest one that is set wins: the thread's own choice, else
  its project's, else the machine's. One function in contracts
  (threadAnswersLinkedQuestions) computes it; the server reads it through
  readAnsweringThread for t3_ask_send and the last gate before an answering
  turn starts, t3_ask_list filters with it, and web and mobile show it.
- Machine level: new server setting linkedQuestionsAnswerByDefault (default
  false), beside linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess: same schema file,
  changing it needs access:write, a row "Answer linked questions in every
  thread" in Settings -> Environments -> List under "Links into this
  machine", manageable and lockable through the managed policy. A lock fixes
  the machine level only. No mobile settings row.
- Project level: not set, on or off, stored in the server's project row
  (projection_projects.linked_questions, carried by project.meta-updated) and
  changed only by the new project mutation project.linked-questions.set,
  which needs orchestration:operate and access:write. It has no MCP tool,
  palette entry or keybinding; t3_project_create and t3_project_update do
  not take it, and no t3.json or project setting carries it. The column is
  added when the project store starts, not by a numbered migration, so a
  database keeps upstream's migration ids; when two processes add it at the
  same first start, a "duplicate column name" answer counts as added.
  Entry: "Answer linked questions" in the project folder's menu in the web
  sidebar (the only web sidebar menu with project actions); it writes every
  checkout of the project. The mobile app has no project menu, so it has no
  entry.
- Thread level: thread.linked-questions.set and its "Answer linked
  questions" entry keep their place and permissions; enabled is now true,
  false or null (not set). Off is stored as linkedQuestionsDisabledAt
  beside linkedQuestionsEnabledAt.
- The thread and project controls (web thread header and sidebar menus, the
  web sidebar project folder menu, a row in the project's settings page,
  the mobile thread row) offer
  "Default (on|off, from the project|this machine)", "On" and "Off", and
  Default returns a thread or project to not set.
- Question threads, subagents, side chats and a workflow run's agent steps
  never answer at any level, are left out of t3_ask_list (workflow steps
  already in its query) and no longer offer the entry. A task delegated from
  a linked machine keeps its menu entry and follows the levels.
- Off on a thread means, on this machine: the thread is not listed and
  cannot be asked; its own rows are never read to build an answer, also not
  through a fork; and a thread made from it afterwards starts off. It does
  not reach back: a thread that already exists keeps its own choice, title
  and own rows. One rule (isLinkedQuestionsOff) decides whether a thread is
  off now: its own choice, or, for a side chat or subagent, which has no
  menu, the choice of the nearest thread above it that has one. Side chats
  and subagents get no choice of their own on this build; an off stored on
  one by an older build or client still counts for its forks.
- With that rule: a fork of an off thread starts off; every other fork starts
  not set, never on. A thread an agent starts through create_threads or
  t3_thread_launch on this machine starts off when the calling thread is
  off, with or without a first message: the server puts the calling thread on
  thread.create as startedFromThreadId, which a client's thread.create
  cannot carry and which can only ever make a thread off. A linked
  question's context stops its walk up the fork ancestors at one that is
  off, as it stops at a /clear, also when it was switched off after the
  fork; what a fork shows its owner, ordinary handoffs and fork history are
  unchanged.
- A thread an off thread's agent starts on a linked machine, through
  delegate_task or through t3_thread_launch with environmentId, starts off
  there. Every forwarded t3_thread_launch goes through one decision in
  PeerForwarding: when the calling thread is off by isLinkedQuestionsOff (a
  side chat or subagent by the thread above it), the launch carries
  t3_thread_launch's new optional linkedQuestionsOff (true only, so it only
  narrows), sent to a machine that advertises linkedQuestionLevels or whose
  capabilities cannot be read; the other machine passes it to thread.create.
  An agent may pass it itself. A machine on a build without that capability
  has no machine or project level, so a thread there answers only once
  switched on, and it would refuse the unknown argument; release 20261125.1
  advertises linkedQuestionLevels but refuses the argument too, so a launch or
  delegation from an off thread to it fails. When the other machine's
  capabilities cannot be read, the argument is sent, so an older machine
  refuses that launch and the agent can retry. No other forwarded tool starts
  a new top-level thread for the calling thread: create_threads is not
  forwarded, a t3_thread_transfer fork there follows its source thread's
  choice as a fork here does, and threads that run_scheduled_task_now or a
  scheduled task starts follow the levels.
- Stored data: a thread stored as on stays on; every other thread and every
  project starts not set. A thread switched off on a build without levels
  stays off, also after a rollback to such a build and the next upgrade. Such
  a build stores off only by clearing linkedQuestionsEnabledAt, drops
  linkedQuestionsDisabledAt whenever it writes a thread, and its events are
  pruned after a day; command receipts are kept. This build writes the choice
  into the receipt's command type (thread.linked-questions.set:on, :off or
  :default); builds before this one (those without levels, and release
  20261125.1) wrote plain thread.linked-questions.set, and on a build without
  levels any accepted command that left linkedQuestionsEnabledAt null was an
  off. On every store start, a thread with no stored choice, no
  linkedQuestionsDisabledAt key and whose newest accepted such receipt by
  result_sequence is plain or :off gets linkedQuestionsDisabledAt set to that
  receipt's time; a thread returned to Default on this build stays Default.
  One UPDATE ... FROM, outside the numbered migrations, starts from the
  receipts (through idx_orch_command_receipts_aggregate), so only threads
  with such a receipt have their payload read, skips rows whose payload is
  not valid JSON, and runs in one transaction with the metadata row
  linked-questions-off-repaired in orchestration_v2_projection_metadata,
  which each successful run refreshes for the rollout pipeline and which
  gates nothing. If the repair fails, it logs a warning naming what that
  means and the store starts. Until a repair succeeds in the server process,
  only a thread's own on answers: t3_ask_list, t3_ask_send and the turn-start
  gate treat the project and machine levels as not set, and an answer is
  built without rows inherited from a fork's ancestors. Each of them tries
  the repair again before it reads a thread
  (ProjectionStoreV2.linkedQuestionsOffRepaired), one attempt at a time and
  not sooner than 30 seconds after a failed one ended. Clients keep showing
  the stored choices.
- The header chip "Answers linked questions" and the mobile thread row
  (the menu's subtitle) show the effective state.
- t3_ask_list returns the newest activity first, at most 50 threads, each
  with its project's name, takes an optional titleContains
  (case-insensitive, thread title or project name), and says how many
  matched (total) and whether the list was cut (truncated). Archived
  threads stay out; scheduled-task webhook tokens are withheld from project
  names as from titles. t3_ask_send and the turn-start gate use the same
  effective state, so a question that has not started is refused when
  answering is off at any level; at both turn-start gates, through one
  helper, a project or setting that cannot be read refuses too.
- Mixed builds: servers advertise a new capability linkedQuestionLevels
  beside askLinks. Web and mobile offer the project entry and the
  three-choice thread control only with it; against a server without it the
  thread keeps the previous On/Off switch and the project entry and the
  machine setting's row are absent.
  A t3_ask_list reply from a machine on the previous build decodes with an
  empty project, total = the threads returned and truncated = false.
- Limits, the answering turn, question threads, the inbound cap and link
  levels are unchanged; what an answer sees changes only by leaving out an
  off ancestor's rows, and every inherited row until the repair has
  succeeded. User docs explain the three
  levels, name the managed-policy key and state what off covers: not listed,
  not asked, its messages never read for an answer, threads made from it
  start off, and a fork made earlier is switched off separately; two
  internal sentences that said "opt-in" now say "answers".

Base: upstream 54ba2408f4 with the series through 12b09ce160 (on it a
question's context stops at the asked thread's latest /clear and ask links
are refused writesOwnerWork); unchanged by this commit.

Not covered:
- The evals cases were built with --dry-run but not run against a model (no API key here), also not for t3_thread_launch's new linkedQuestionsOff argument.
- A project group spanning machines shows the first checkout's choice in its menu.
- The mobile project overview (Organize section) has no row for the project level.
- Titles and own messages of threads that existed before the switch stay; replies that repeat an off thread are their own words.
- A fork made in an off fork at a row inherited from a thread that is not off starts not set.
- Text an agent of an off thread sends into existing threads, scheduled tasks and workflow steps is left out of an answer only as that message.
- Threads a scheduled task launches follow the levels, also when an off thread's agent made the task.
- An off stored on a side chat or subagent by an older build or client cannot be cleared from a menu; each fork of it can be changed.
- No test for the launch sent while the other machine's capabilities cannot be read, and none that concurrent calls share one repair attempt.
- An off a thread got by being made from an off thread has no receipt and is lost when a build without this feature rewrites the thread.
- A launch or delegation from an off thread to a machine on 20261125.1 fails.
- While the repair has not succeeded, only a thread's own on answers, and a thread made from a thread whose off is not restored yet starts not set.
- A Default set on release 20261125.1 (plain receipt) reads as off once a build without levels has rewritten the thread.
- A fork an off thread's agent makes of another thread, here or on a linked machine, follows that thread's choice.
- The repair reads every thread receipt on each store start (about 20 ms per 100,000); receipts have no index on command_type.

Port to b8be2287dc: imports only, plus the measured budget. Sidebar.tsx imports upstream 78de041d48's (#24917, settle and snooze threads while their machine is offline) isThreadTriageAction next to this commit's linkedQuestionsEnabledFor; thread-list-v2-items.tsx keeps upstream's useEnvironmentScopeDenied next to this commit's useEnvironmentServerConfig and useProject. agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes is 63302 as AgentContextBudget.test.ts measures it on the carried tree. No upstream migration up to b8be2287dc rebuilds projection_projects, so the linked_questions column is still added at store start. The change itself is unchanged. commandPermissions.test.ts keeps passing upstream's shell status atom (live) as createThreadEnvironmentAtoms's third argument.

Port to 221953fbf3: useThreadActionMenu.ts: upstream 95411e3cd7 (#40742, thread actions for the current thread from cmd+k) builds the menu in readMenu; readThreadLinkedQuestionsMenu(thread) replaces linkedQuestionsEnabled in readMenu's buildThreadActionMenuItems call, and the linked-questions cases run in its run(action) with readMenu's thread, so upstream's cmd+k rows for the current thread offer the same Default, On and Off choices. agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes is 63443 as AgentContextBudget.test.ts measures it on the carried tree. No upstream migration up to 221953fbf3 (the new one is 117_WorkflowMailSender, which adds workflow_mail.sender_json) touches projection_projects, so the linked_questions column is still added at store start. The change itself is unchanged.

Port to 0085217d1b: agentContextBudget.json: mcpToolListJsonBytes recomputed with AgentContextBudget.test.ts on the new base (63528, the ask-only links value 63338 plus this commit's 190, the same growth as before). Mobile useThreadListActions.ts: the useStableCallback wrapper the ask-only links port added (upstream 2ae731530d, #43024) takes enabled as boolean | null, as this commit's setThreadLinkedQuestions does. The change itself is otherwise unchanged.

